title The name spent on every read, and the ceiling that rose to allow it
prompt The baton, the card, and DIFFUSER's grant -- mantra/src/store.rye names a blob by its SHA3-256 digest and no read path recomputes it, beside a read that fills its buffer and calls that the answer. Four ceilings, none named, two heights over one directory. Four lines repair it, yours patchouli.
rota lap 4364, row 4 -- Earth, the row that breathes in. Threshold plus the clock-and-the-mark and the marked value. Earth's instruction is to take the concrete fact in at the door before arguing with it, and it decided the lap twice. First against my own cold pass: it reported six reds, and rather than reasoning about them I re-ran each on the tree that actually stands -- four were stale, the tree having moved under the run. Second in the repair itself: a bound is the concrete fact at a function's door, and four of them stood unnamed.
think Reds first, so the six cold reds came before the grant. Re-running them was the whole finding: declared_ceiling, reds_ledger_headline, reds_pin_capacity and fold_shelf_link_repoint were all GREEN on the current tree, because tree_moved=yes -- a round-open pulled a peer's REDS fold in mid-pass, taking construction/REDS.md from 43,634 bytes to 39,212. A transcript is a reading of a moment, and a moment that has passed.
think stash_record held at unlanded=5. Five session logs of mine, 20260907.224502 through 20260908.143034, stood in three round-open stashes and nowhere on disk. I restored them with git checkout stash@{N} -- <path>, reading the TREE rather than the blob so the mode survives, and left the account shelves and REDS fold shelves parked: their ITINERARY blocks are superseded and their fold rows would have created a shelf nothing recites.
think Reading 20260908.143034 was the lap's turn. That parked log had ported main.rye onto the weave model, DECLINED the Store on purpose -- adopting a module whose ceiling was 1 MiB against the CLI's 4 would quarter a shipped CLI's blob capacity -- and closed on the words the nearer mechanical door in this lane is the two Store ceilings. I was one command from repeating a lap sitting in my own stash, which is DIFFUSER's own sentence from this morning, in the room next door.
think The repair had to raise the ceiling rather than pick one of the two, or the port would land the very regression the parked lap refused. max_blob_bytes = 1 << 27 is derived: a mantra-weave-v1 record is one row per line, so 128 MiB over weave.max_weave_lines is 128 bytes a row, against this tree's own 50.3 bytes a line measured with git ls-files '*.rye' | xargs wc -lc. It is 32 times the CLI's own 4 MiB, so naming one number raised both.
think The read now goes through readFileAlloc under an Io.Limit of max_blob_bytes + 1 rather than readFile into a fixed buffer. The plus one is load-bearing: readFileAlloc refuses on REACHING its limit, so a blob standing exactly on the ceiling still reads whole. Then Sha3.hash over the bytes returned, hex-encoded, compared to the name asked for -- BlobNameMismatch when they disagree. A verified read can never return a prefix.
obs The proof had to be a running one, and it was the sharpest thing in the lap. Two binaries in one pen, the same five commands: diff -r over both .mantra/ trees identical, messages identical. Then truncate the weave blob by 20 bytes: the elder CLI prints mantra status 2 added, 1 removed with a half-line - b and exits 0, reporting a diff against a corrupted history to a user who has no way to know. The ported one refuses BlobNameMismatch and exits 1.
obs The witness proves the 128 MiB ceiling in its REFUSING direction at both edges, on a real allocation and a real file, and says in its own header that it does not prove the exact boundary -- two SHA3-256 passes over 128 MiB every lap buys less than it costs. The control names the same gap from its own side: moving the limit to max_blob_bytes would pass every phase. A weakness named in two places is a weakness a reader can act on.
think Landing the five logs was not one move but five, and each was a standing guard telling me something true. log_file_claim refuses a staged log naming a path in neither tree nor index, so the two account shelves, the three REDS fold shelves and the whole parked rish_report_bound guard family had to land with them; I re-proved that guard on metal at 31.7s before writing its roster row, rather than trusting the parked lap's own 19s.
think Then reds_spine_derive named three squatters: rows %594, %600 and %640 were booked from a local read while those laps sat parked, and xy has since spent all three numbers on other stamps. The guard printed the repair, so the three shelves are renamed and renumbered, each carrying an erratum saying the stamp never moved and the view was reallocated. Nothing landed cites the old paths; the landed testimony that mentions those numbers names entirely different shelves, which is the collision itself.
think And the renumber had to run TWICE, which is the law's own argument arriving in one round. My first pass derived above %644 as %647; between my commit and my rebase a peer published rows binding %645 and %646, so the guard read two squatters again and the three landed at %647, %648 and %649. A number read from a tree is allocated per tree, and no amount of care avoids that -- which is why the stamp is the key and the number is a view.
think Two more followed. fold_shelf_link_repoint found four links inside the restored account shelves written at the card's depth rather than the shelf's, and applied the repair it had already computed. shell_dialect_touch found three sed -i sites in the restored control -- GNU-only, and the dialect law was seated after that lap parked -- so they now call sed_inplace from shell_portable.sh.
obs Landing a parked lap is not a checkout. Five guards had to speak before the testimony could stand, and every one of them was right; the work of recovery is mostly reconciling a lap written against a tree that has moved.
obs The card cost eight trims. ITINERARY stood at 40,802 of 40,960 when I opened it, and my honest account was 2,300 bytes against an elder block of 1,398; it was trimmed eight times to 40,960, then trimmed again after the rebase brought peers' longer blocks in, landing at 40,941 -- 19 bytes of headroom. Four ships have flagged this shape from two rooms today and it is worse each time.
obs Against myself: tame_style_check named my compound assert rather than my own reading of the rule, and I had read the rule this lap.
